      "input_checks_unchanged": true,
      "proof": "If all n first-symbol equivalence classes under < are distinct, every pair of distinct suffixes differs at its first symbol, so their order is the sorted order of first symbols. No empty suffix is returned.",
      "integral": "Sort/unique is consistent for built-in integral types, including bool and signed/unsigned extrema. Every input occurs exactly once in alphabet, so lower_bound is a rank in [0,n), and inverse-rank writes form a fully initialized output permutation.",
      "nonintegral": "Strict weak order makes incomparability equivalence; after sorting, the existing rank loop counts exactly these classes. alphabet==n means no equivalent first symbols, so existing sorted positions is the answer. T is never copied and == is not newly required.",
      "empty": "Integral returns empty result directly; nonintegral returns empty positions after its existing empty rank loop. n==1 returns [0].",
      "bounds": "Existing suffix_length_check remains before either branch and is not skipped. n<INT_MAX-1 makes all int indices, rank increments, and int(n) safe. lower_bound cannot return end because alphabet retains every distinct input.",
      "ownership": "Returned result/positions are owning vectors; input is const and unchanged. Existing alphabet/encoded buffers are destroyed by RAII on every exception and successful return. Eliminating possible bad_alloc sites is not a contract regression.",
      "complexity": "No new validation scan. Integral inverse-rank lower_bound loop replaces the old encoding lower_bound loop; nonintegral reuses its already sorted positions and rank count. O(n log n) time/O(n) memory bounds maintained.",
      "performance_note": "Duplicate paths gain only one O(1) comparison; generated code/inlining can still move, so parent should measure large/small duplicates and unique vectors. String-only Verify should not instantiate these changed templates; text correspondence is a codegen check for parent."
    }
